          <h3>Open consoles</h3>
          <p>
            Bowflex’s JRNY console is the clearest example among established brands. Our <a href="/bowflex-t16-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T16 review</a> records Bowflex listing built-in apps including Netflix, Prime Video, YouTube, Spotify and a web
            browser, and the Amazon listing names Netflix, Prime Video and Spotify. The SYTIRY goes further on
            paper, with a listing that says Chrome comes preinstalled and that you can download any other app
            you want. An open console behaves most like a tablet, which is exactly what most people asking
            this question want.
          </p>
          <h3>Approved-list consoles</h3>
          <p>
            Aviron names a long list (Netflix, Hulu, Disney+, Prime Video, YouTube, Spotify and Apple Music)
            but does not mention a browser or an app store in the listing, so treat the named list as the
            whole list. You can watch those services; you cannot add others.
          </p>
          <h3>Membership-gated consoles</h3>
          <p>
            iFIT consoles on NordicTrack and ProForm are locked to iFIT’s own software. Netflix, Prime Video
            and a few others run inside iFIT on the top Pro plan, and there is no browser and no way to add
            an app of your choosing. Our <a href="/can-you-watch-netflix-on-nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to watching Netflix on a NordicTrack</a> lists the apps iFIT offers, the ones it does not, and why the old workarounds are a bad idea.
          </p>
          <h3>Unnamed streaming</h3>
          <p>
            SOLE’s and Sunny’s listings both promise streaming over Wi-Fi without naming a single service.
            That is not a reason to rule them out, but it is a reason to ask the seller, or the maker’s
            support line, to confirm the one app you care about before you order a 200 lb box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="membership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Do You Need a Membership to Watch Netflix on a Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            You always need your own Netflix account. Whether you also need the treadmill maker’s
            membership depends on the brand, and it is the single biggest running cost on this page.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack and ProForm (iFIT): yes.</strong> The T Series 10 listing says that with
            an iFIT Pro Membership, listed at $39 a month, you can stream your favourite shows and movies,
            and marks streaming as “iFIT Pro Membership Required”. Without it the treadmill works as a
            manual machine with the touchscreen showing your stats, but the streaming apps are not
            available. Our <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT guide</a> covers what else the plan includes and whether it earns its cost.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bowflex: no.</strong> Bowflex says the T16 works without a JRNY subscription, and the
            streaming apps run with your own accounts. A JRNY membership adds Bowflex’s adaptive workouts and
            classes, which you may or may not want.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Aviron: no, for streaming.</strong> The listing says the membership, sold separately,
            “unlocks the full experience with interactive and community-driven features”. Our <a href="/aviron-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Aviron review</a> records Aviron
            saying the membership is not required and that streaming apps such as Netflix and YouTube remain
            available without it; the games, classes and scenic routes need it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>SOLE: no membership is mentioned.</strong> The F89 listing pairs the screen with Sole’s
            SOLE+ app, which our Sole reviews record as free. <strong>Sunny and SYTIRY</strong> do not state
            a membership for streaming; Sunny’s listing describes its SunnyFit app’s trainer-led workouts
            separately, so ask Sunny whether those need a plan.
          </p>
          <p>
            Over five years, $39 a month is more than $2,300 before any price rise, which is more than the
            gap between the NordicTrack and the Bowflex. If streaming is the main reason you want a screen and
            you would not use iFIT’s classes, the no-membership machines cost less over their life even when
            the sticker price is higher.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tablet-route" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is a Tablet Holder Cheaper Than a Streaming Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Usually, by a long way. A tablet or phone on a holder runs every app you already pay for,
            including the ones no treadmill console names (Disney+ on most consoles, Max, Peacock, Paramount+,
            Plex, your own downloads), and it keeps working after the treadmill maker stops updating its
            software. A good holder costs a fraction of the price gap between a screen treadmill and a
            plain one, and our <a href="/best-treadmill-tablet-holders/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tablet holder picks</a> cover the ones that clamp securely to a console.
          </p>
          <p>
            The trade-offs are real but small for most viewers. A tablet is smaller than a 16 or 22-inch
            console, it sits wherever the holder puts it rather than at the ideal height, and it does not
            control the treadmill. A wall-mounted TV in front of the treadmill, covered in our <a href="/best-treadmill-tv-stands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill TV stand guide</a>,
            solves the size problem for the price of a mount.
          </p>
