              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Membership-gated (iFIT)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iFIT’s approved apps</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 in. and up</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iFIT Pro plus your streaming plans</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tablet on a holder</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Anything your tablet runs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Your tablet’s</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Your streaming plans only</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TV on a wall or stand</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Anything your TV runs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">As big as the wall allows</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Your streaming plans only</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The tablet route is the right answer for anyone who mainly wants a good treadmill and something
            to watch. A built-in screen earns its price when you also want trainer-led workouts that change
            the speed and incline for you, or when the convenience of one device matters more than the money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="screen-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Screen Size Do You Need for Watching TV?</h2>
<p>
            Bigger than you think, because you are watching from a moving, bouncing position at arm’s
            length or further. A 10-inch screen is comfortable for following stats and class prompts and
            fine for a sitcom at a walk; for a film or for subtitles at a jog, most people find it small.
            Sixteen inches is where watching starts to feel like television rather than a phone, and the
            22 and 24-inch consoles on the Aviron and SOLE are the closest a treadmill gets to a small TV.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two other screen features matter more for viewing than for workouts. <strong>Tilt and swivel</strong> let you angle the screen to your eye line and, on the SOLE F89’s swivel screen, turn it toward a
            mat for off-treadmill classes. The NordicTrack T Series 10’s screen tilts, per its listing. <strong>Speakers</strong> are rarely good enough for dialogue over a belt and motor, so plan on
            Bluetooth <a href="/best-headphones-for-treadmill-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>headphones</a>; Bowflex, SOLE, Sunny and SYTIRY all list speakers or Bluetooth, and a
            headphone connection matters more than speaker wattage.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, think about where the screen will be in five years. A treadmill motor and deck often
            outlast the console software, and a screen that stops receiving app updates may lose a streaming
            service that changes its requirements. That risk is higher with small online brands, whose
            listings do not say how long the console software will be supported, and it is another point in
            favour of a tablet you can replace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is It Safe to Watch TV on a Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Yes, at the right pace and with the safety key clipped on. Watching something gripping draws
            your eyes and attention away from the belt, and the most common way to come off a treadmill is a
            drift to the side or the back of the deck while distracted. Three habits help.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Watch at a steady pace.</strong> Save intervals and fast running for music or a class, and keep films and series for walking, incline walking and easy jogging.</li>
            <li><strong>Clip the safety key to your clothing</strong> every time, so the belt stops if you drift too far back.</li>
            <li><strong>Put the screen in front of you at eye level</strong>, not to one side, so you are not twisting your neck or stepping off line to see it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            This is also why incline walking pairs so well with streaming: a steady 3 mph uphill walk is a
            hard workout that leaves your attention free for a programme. If that is how you plan to use the
            treadmill, our <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline treadmills</a> guide covers the machines built for it, several of which pair well with a tablet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-you-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Check Before You Buy a Streaming Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Amazon listings for screen treadmills are written to sell the screen, and the details that
            decide whether you will actually watch Netflix on it are often missing. Five checks cover most of
            the risk.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Find the named app.</strong> If the listing does not name the service you want, ask the seller in writing. “Streaming” and “entertainment” do not mean Netflix.</li>
            <li><strong>Find the membership line.</strong> Look for “membership required” next to streaming in the listing bullets, and price the plan over five years.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the Wi-Fi where the treadmill will stand.</strong> A garage or basement with weak signal will make any streaming console frustrating; a mesh point or a wired connection may be needed.</li>
            <li><strong>Check which version you are buying.</strong> Several of these listings have older versions with different consoles, and an older console may run different software from the one described.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the return terms.</strong> If the app you wanted is missing when the treadmill arrives, a freight return is slow and can be expensive. Know who pays before you order.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Do not rely on unofficial workarounds. Owners of locked consoles have long shared ways to open them
            up, but makers close these routes in updates, and using them can break the terms of the software
            licence and, in some cases, cause problems with support. If an open screen matters, buy a treadmill
            whose maker says it has one, or use a tablet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Before You Buy a Treadmill for Netflix: 4 Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
          <p>Each of these comes from trusting the word “streaming” in a listing instead of checking what it means.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming “streaming” means Netflix</h3>
              <p>Many listings use streaming to mean workout video or an unnamed set of apps. Only buy for Netflix if the listing or the maker names Netflix, and ask the seller in writing if it does not.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Leaving the membership out of the price</h3>
              <p>On iFIT machines Netflix needs iFIT Pro, listed at $39 a month for the T Series 10. Over five years that is more than $2,300, which can make a cheaper treadmill the more expensive one.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying for a screen you could replace with a tablet</h3>
              <p>If you would not use the maker’s classes, a plain treadmill and a secure tablet holder give you every app for far less, and the tablet can be replaced when it ages.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Watching at running pace</h3>
              <p>A gripping film pulls your attention off the belt. Keep streaming for walking, incline walking and easy jogging, clip the safety key on every time, and set the screen straight ahead at eye level.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For most people the best treadmill for Netflix is the Bowflex T16</strong>: its listing
            names Netflix, Prime Video and Spotify, Bowflex adds YouTube and a web browser, and it works without
            a fitness membership, on a 22 by 60-inch deck with a 375 lb limit. If Disney+ or YouTube matter as
            much as Netflix, the Aviron Victory names the longest app list; if you want iFIT’s classes anyway, the
            NordicTrack T Series 10 streams Netflix on the Pro plan for the lowest machine price from an
            established brand.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before any of them, ask whether you need a streaming screen at all. A tablet on a secure holder runs
            every app you pay for, needs no treadmill membership and costs a fraction of the price gap. If that
            suits you, start with a machine from our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmills without subscriptions</a> list and add one of our <a href="/best-treadmill-tablet-holders/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">tablet holder picks</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

